    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "Do you often feel very anxious when you have to interact with other people because you worry they might judge you?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Anxious about people judging me? No, no, I don't think so. I mean, I interact with people all day, right? As a support specialist, I'm constantly on the phone or on video calls with clients, or talking to the other techs on the floor. And honestly, I'm usually the one who knows what's going on—technically speaking, I mean. I know the systems inside and out. So when I'm talking to a client whose server just crashed, I'm not worried about them judging *me* as a person, you know? I'm just focused on the fact that I need to fix their problem. \n\nSure, I get stressed if I'm taking too long because I kept losing my train of thought while checking their firewall configurations, but that's more about me being frustrated with myself, like I said before. It's not this deep-seated fear that they're looking at me and thinking I'm a weirdo or something. I actually like talking to people, for the most part. Sometimes I probably talk too much, to be honest. I'll get a client on the phone and start explaining the entire architecture of their network when all they wanted to know was why their email wasn't syncing. But no, I don't get that freezing, panicky feeling about being judged. I'm 53 years old, I've been doing this a long time, I know who I am. I just... I just wish my brain would stay on the track I put it on, instead of constantly derailing when I'm trying to work. But social anxiety? No, I wouldn't say I have that at all.",
